Using Vieta's formulas, we have $
\begin{equation*}
\left\{
\begin{array}{rcl}
\alpha +\beta &=& -p\\
\alpha\beta &=& 1\\
\gamma +\delta &=&-q\\
\gamma \delta &=& 1
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation*}
$ Let $a = (\alpha - \gamma)(\alpha + \delta).$ We have: $a = \alpha^2 + \alpha \delta - \alpha\gamma - \gamma \delta$ = $-p\alpha + \alpha \delta - \alpha \gamma$ i.e., $a = -p\alpha +\delta \alpha - \gamma \alpha$ i.e., $a = \alpha (\delta - \gamma - p).$ We have: $a = \alpha^2 + \alpha\delta - \alpha\gamma - \gamma\delta.$ We have: $a = \alpha^2 - \alpha(c + \gamma +\delta) - \gamma \delta =\alpha^2 - \alpha(-q) - 1,$ i.e., $a = \alpha^2 + IQ we write: because $a = \alpha^2 + \alpha \delta - \alpha \gamma - \gamma \delta,$ we then we have:$a(\alpha + q) = \alpha^2 \delta - \alpha \gamma \delta$ i.e., $a(\alpha + q) = a$ Because $a \neq 0 a = \frac{1}{\alpha+q} - \alpha^2 + \alpha(-q)$ i.e., $a = 1 - 2\alpha (\alpha + q) = 1 - 2(pq -1) = 3 -2pq.$</think> <answer> $3 - 2pq$ </answer>

